Two decisions from the editor at 13:00 (his words in the brief above; please read it whole).
PS1, the positioning: the book is not about the presentation; the talk is a side effect, as a book about how a music festival was set up is not about the bands. The story is how a creator (him here; it could be anyone) was given a factory of agents, a team assembled fast around one objective and taken apart at the end, built on earlier experiments and flows; it gave him a huge feedback loop and control of the medium (his slides were a compromise he talked over; here he pressed play, and fine-tuned round after round). Ideas he names: inventing on principle, the factory of agents, Wardley maps' commoditisation, Kevin Kelly's evolution of ideas. A story arc and a practical book: read it and learn to do it, here and in the making of this book. Readers he names: the Web Summit team he is assembling (each with their own agents), people building their own newsroom, the Vibathon's finishers.
ZM1, the book as a zoom: editions at about 1, 3, 10, 30, 70 and 150 pages, so a reader starts small and zooms in. Now: the 3- and 10-page editions (then 15 or 30), told by the cast (NR1).
The cover (CV1) and the title (NS1) stay open; both now sit on PS1.
Yours: from the record, what the day's factory was built on: which earlier projects, tools and flows it reused (the PHP newsroom, the Gen ZX site, the key tools, the wake protocol), each with its commit or page, so the book can show one project's work used in the next.

